  "teamId": 114,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Cleveland Guardians opened the year with a .500 record through March and April, a team still finding its footing. The early weeks were defined as much by bullpen vulnerabilities as by flashes of offensive promise — Cade Smith surrendered critical hits in back-to-back games to open the series against Seattle, and Connor Brogdon gave up a three-run shot that swung a pivotal April 17th game. But even in those stumbles, the Guardians answered: Daniel Schneemann's grand slam earlier that same evening and Brayan Rocchio's three-run blast the following night signaled that this offense could punch back.\n\nMay was the turning point that saved the season. Cleveland surged to an 18-11 record, its best month of the year, riding timely hitting and improved run prevention. Kyle Manzardo delivered a go-ahead homer in May that captured the month's spirit — a team that had learned to grind out wins. The summer months were bumpier. June brought a 10-15 skid as the bullpen was torched repeatedly, most painfully when Cade Smith was tagged for a walk-off single that cost Cleveland a game late in the month. July and August hovered near .500, the team treading water in the standings, needing something to spark a charge.\n\nThat spark came in September. Brayan Rocchio, quietly one of the season's most important performers, delivered a walk-off double on September 15th that represented the Guardians at their best — young, clutch, and hungry. Cleveland went 16-9 in the final month to lock up a playoff berth at 85-77, a final sprint that erased the summer doldrums and carried genuine momentum into October.\n\nThe postseason has arrived, and the Guardians have already cleared their first hurdle, dispatching their ALDS opponent behind José Ramírez's thunder and Jo Adell's surprising heroics. Ramírez homered twice in the series, including a two-run shot that announced Cleveland's intentions loudly. Adell, meanwhile, is tripping the wire twice with clutch triples at exactly the right moments. The Guardians are still playing, still fighting, and whatever comes next in this October run is still being written.",
